WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:03.000
- Let's talk about the most common mistake

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.790
that people make on
law school final exams.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:09.350
This thing actually got
you A's in undergrad,

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:12.110
but it will get you an F in Law School.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:13.820
Coming out of undergrad, everyone has

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:15.530
this mindset and you'll have to work

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:18.806
very hard to avoid it, so stick around.

00:00:18.806 --> 00:00:21.389
(upbeat music)

00:00:23.681 --> 00:00:27.310
If you came straight out
of a college class on law

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:30.120
and went straight into
a law school final exam,

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.650
you would feel like you
were crushing the exam

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.930
but really you'd be getting zero points.

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:38.580
That's because undergrad
teaches us that the way

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.760
to get all of the points on an exam

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.966
is to regurgitate all of
the information you can

00:00:43.966 --> 00:00:47.180
to show the professor that
you remembered everything

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.030
that they talked about in class.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:52.040
This is the old information dump.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:53.960
It works great in undergrad,

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.800
but it doesn't work at all in law school.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.870
Law school exams use the issue spotter

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:03.370
hypothetical method of final exams.

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:05.240
Regardless of the class that you take,

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:08.780
your professor most likely
will give you a set of facts

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:11.730
whether it's one paragraph
or on several pages

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:14.780
where bad things happen
to a group of people.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:17.510
Things go wrong, people get murdered,

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:21.280
people try and take possession
of another piece of property.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.270
This is the hypothetical facts

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:25.430
that you're going to have to deal with.

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:28.230
And when you get an issue spotter exam,

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:32.900
it's your job to figure out
who can sue whom for what.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.670
These are the issues
that you have to spot,

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.410
that's why they call it an issue spotter.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:39.430
So how are you going to do that?

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:41.400
Well you use the law.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.900
But the thing is that knowing

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:47.750
the law is only the first
step, it's the table stakes.

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:50.640
If it's a contracts class
and you simply regurgitate

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.860
the entire history of
contract law onto the page,

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:56.760
you're gonna get zero points.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.300
Your contracts professor
is not looking for you

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:02.860
to tell him or her the
history of contract law.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:04.740
Your professor already knows that.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.640
Your professor doesn't
care if you know the law.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:11.060
Your professor wants you to use the law.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.130
Now imagine if you took a calculus exam

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:15.920
and instead of answering
the math question,

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.060
you just simply talked about the history

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:21.350
of how Newton-Leibniz invented calculus.

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:23.490
How many points do you
think you would get?

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:25.130
Probably not many.

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:28.680
On a calculus exam, you are
supposed to use the concepts

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:33.540
of calculus and determine
the answer to the formula.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:38.090
And just like in grade school,
showing your work matters.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:40.870
In fact, showing your work matters more

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:42.550
than the actual answer itself,

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:44.950
and it's the same way in law school.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:48.600
The information dump,
the most common mistake

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.900
that people make on law school exams

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:53.020
worked perfectly in college

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:56.040
but it doesn't work at all in law school.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.950
Law school is all about applying the law,

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:02.360
applying the concept
of the causes of action

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:04.910
and the premises to the facts themselves.

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:06.660
It's all about the application.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:09.880
And here's the rub,
unlike elementary school

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.660
and in most of college, where there is

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:17.433
an actual right answer, in law
school it is all gray area.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.950
The math example that I
used isn't 100% accurate

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:25.300
because in calculus there is a
right answer to the question,

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.480
so showing your work is important

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:30.010
but getting the right
answer is also important.

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:33.450
But in law school, there
is no right answer.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:37.610
You have to show that
you could use the law

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:40.200
and the facts together, synthesize them,

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:43.050
and act as an advocate for one party,

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:44.992
and of course you also have to show

00:03:44.992 --> 00:03:46.130
that you are smart enough to know

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:47.810
all the counterarguments
that the other side

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:49.740
is going to use and go back and forth

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:52.000
between arguments and counterarguments.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.230
The worst thing that you could possibly do

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:57.400
is to just write down all
the information that you

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:00.110
can think of, everything that
was talked about in class,

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:02.930
that will get you zero
points on a final exam,

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:06.040
unlike college where it
probably got you an A plus.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:08.840
So don't go into law school
with the same mindset

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:10.610
that all of your classmates have,

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:13.300
that what got them to law
school is the same thing

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:15.170
that's going to get
them A's in law school,

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:16.720
it's just simply not true.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.810
If you try to use an information dump,

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:20.070
your professor will hate you

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:22.410
and give you a terrible grade on the exam.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:25.250
You have to apply the
law, show that you can

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:28.180
think like a lawyer,
and here at Legal Eagle,

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:30.160
that's our entire mission is to show you

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.340
how to actually think like a lawyer

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:33.800
because you certainly didn't learn

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:35.370
to think like a lawyer in college

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:37.870
and sadly, even law professors

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:39.250
aren't very good at teaching you

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:40.970
how to think like a lawyer.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:42.690
So go in with the right mindset,

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:45.280
cast aside what you learned in undergrad,

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:48.600
and go in and crush your
next law school exam.

